answers.text:
Call `e.preventDefault()` in your `onSubmit` handler, then read the values with `new FormData(e.target)`.

By default, the browser sends the form data to the current URL and refreshes the page, so `e.preventDefault()` overrides that behavior. `new FormData(e.target)` collects every field by its `name`. Give a `name` to every `<input>`, because that name is used as a key in the form data.

```js
export default function Search() {
  function handleSubmit(e) {
    // Prevent the browser from reloading the page
    e.preventDefault();

    // Read the form data
    const form = e.target;
    const formData = new FormData(form);
    const query = formData.get("query");
    alert(`You searched for '${query}'`);
  }

  return (
    <form onSubmit={handleSubmit}>
      <input name="query" />
      <button type="submit">Search</button>
    </form>
  );
}
```

Other ways to use the `FormData`:
- Pass it directly as a fetch body, or convert it to a plain object with `Object.fromEntries(formData.entries())`.
- `Object.fromEntries` doesn't include multiple `<select>` values. To keep them, use `[...formData.entries()]` to get an array of name-value pairs.

If you control an input with state, read from that state on submit rather than from `FormData`.

A pitfall: a `<button>` inside a `<form>` without a `type` attribute will submit it, so use `<button type="button">` for buttons that shouldn't submit.

